# MCP Usage — When and How to Use Each Server

The MCP servers exist to prevent specific failure modes. If a task matches a trigger below
and you skip the server, explain why in the task report.

## Which servers this project uses
- **memory** — DEFAULT: there is **no memory MCP**. The project's Markdown corpus *is* the
  knowledge graph (chronicle + snapshot + knowledge base + INDEX + affects-lookup). OPT-IN: a
  project may enable the `basic-memory` MCP at init — see §1.
- **tracker** — `{{TRACKER}}`. Wired by `/lifecycle-init` at the project's MCP scope (§0,
  default `local` — confined to this repo). `/log-activity --tracker` and `/close-task` branch on
  the tracker chosen at init (Linear, or Atlassian for Jira). If `{{TRACKER}}` is `none`, the
  project runs on the local `TODO.md` only.
- **stack MCPs** — only what the project needs, per the §0 catalog (`slack`, `vercel`,
  `supabase` or `neon`), wired at the same scope. The authoritative enabled list is
  `settings.json → mcp.servers`.
- **github** — optional; the `gh` CLI covers most needs. If a github MCP is used, wire it at
  the §0 scope like everything else — never user-global by assumption.
- **databricks** — `{{DATABRICKS}}`. Data-stack only. **Not an MCP.** When `on`, SQL runs
  through the `databricks-sql-connector` package, authenticated via the `~/.databrickscfg`
  profile `{{DATABRICKS_PROFILE}}`. When `off`, ignore this section.

Enabled servers and connection identity are declared in `.claude/settings.json` (`mcp.scope`,
`mcp.servers`); tokens live in the gitignored `.claude/.local/secrets.env` (the §0 catalog
servers are OAuth — they need no token at all).

---

## 0. MCP scope — where this project's servers are configured

Set at `/lifecycle-init` (C.0) and recorded in committed `.claude/settings.json` →
`"mcp": { "scope": "...", "servers": [...] }`. **Default and framework policy: `local`** — every server lives in
THIS project's entry in `~/.claude.json`, confined to this repo; nothing bleeds into other projects or the
machine's global config, and nothing depends on account-level claude.ai connectors. The three scopes:
`local` = this project's entry in `~/.claude.json` · `project` = the committed `.mcp.json` · `user` =
`~/.claude.json` root. Precedence `local > project > user`, whole-entry override.

**This project's server catalog** (`mcp.servers` is the authoritative enabled list; all are official remote
endpoints with OAuth — no tokens in files, auth completed via `/mcp`, per machine):

| Server | Command |
| --- | --- |
| `linear` (tracker) | `claude mcp add --transport http --scope local linear https://mcp.linear.app/mcp` |
| `atlassian` (tracker, Jira) | `claude mcp add --transport http --scope local atlassian https://mcp.atlassian.com/v1/mcp/authv2` |
| `slack` | `claude mcp add --transport http --scope local slack https://mcp.slack.com/mcp` |
| `vercel` (web stack, Vercel deploy) | `claude mcp add --transport http --scope local vercel https://mcp.vercel.com` |
| `supabase` (DB) | `claude mcp add --transport http --scope local supabase https://mcp.supabase.com/mcp` |
| `neon` (DB — mutually exclusive with supabase) | `claude mcp add --transport http --scope local neon https://mcp.neon.tech/mcp` |

(Commands shown are the default-`local` form. If this project's recorded `mcp.scope` is `project`, the servers
live in the committed `.mcp.json` instead of a `claude mcp add` command; for `user`, swap the `--scope` flag.)

If an endpoint has moved, check the provider's MCP docs and update this table — the repo carries the working
commands. Tool names under each server (`mcp__<name>__*`) can also change between server versions — discover
the exact ones from the session tool list; the tool lists in §3 are indicative. **Databricks is NOT an MCP**:
its connection is CLI/SDK-based via `~/.databrickscfg` profiles (`databricks-sql-connector`, OAuth via
`w.config.authenticate()`) — see the Databricks section below.

**Two-machine consequence:** `local` and `user` scopes DO NOT travel with the repo. On the user's other
machine, `/handshake in` verifies every server in `mcp.servers` exists at the recorded scope and, when missing,
offers the exact command from the table above — it never adds them silently.

**Windows hazard (local scope):** `~/.claude.json` may hold TWO entries for the same project differing
only in drive-letter case (`c:/...` vs `C:/...`); `claude mcp add` writes only the cwd's casing. When
verifying, check both entries and mirror the server into the missing one.

## 1. Memory — the corpus *is* the knowledge graph

**DEFAULT (no MCP):** persistent knowledge lives in the project's Markdown corpus, not in a
memory server. There is **no `search_nodes` / `read_graph` / `create_entities`**. The graph is:
- `docs/project_chronicle.md` (append-only) — decisions & history.
- `docs/context_snapshot.md` — current-state handoff for the next session.
- `docs/knowledge_base/` — durable topic articles (glossary, business rules, gotchas, how-tos),
  curated by the kb-curator at `/close-task` (every Full and Research close; a Transitory one only with a report,
  spec or review).
- `docs/activity_log.jsonl` — per-step machine log.
- `docs/INDEX.md` + `docs/index.json` — queryable index (`type`/`status`/`authority`,
  `depends_on`/`affects` relations).
- `scripts/affects-lookup.mjs` — finds dependents (blast radius) = the graph-walk.

**RECALL — how an agent remembers (session/task start, read before acting):**
- Read `docs/project_chronicle.md` + `docs/context_snapshot.md` + the INDEX (`docs/INDEX.md`).
- For a topic: check `docs/knowledge_base/` for a topic article first (grep titles AND each
  article's `aliases:`), then grep the corpus / read the INDEX, and run
  `node scripts/affects-lookup.mjs <object-id>` (an `affects:` id, not a path) to see what depends on what you're
  about to change.

**RECORD — how an agent persists (after a completed task):** write to the corpus — the task
report (`docs/claude_tasks/reports/`), the reviews, and (via `/close-task`) the append to
`docs/project_chronicle.md`, the overwrite of `docs/context_snapshot.md`, and the kb-curator's
fold into `docs/knowledge_base/`. The corpus is the memory; there is nothing else to write to.

**What to record:** task completion + date + key findings; corrections (wrong paths, broken
assumptions discovered); decisions of record and their rationale; recurring failure modes.

**What NOT to record:** secrets/tokens/credentials; warehouse ids, hostnames, catalog names;
speculation or assumptions; information already captured verbatim in a task report (link,
don't duplicate).

### Opt-in: the `basic-memory` MCP

A project MAY enable a memory MCP at init (`memory_backend = basic-memory`). When enabled:
- **Server name:** `basic-memory`. **Launch:** `uvx basic-memory mcp`.
- **Requires:** `uv`/`uvx` + Python 3.12+ on the machine.
- **Store:** `BASIC_MEMORY_HOME=${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}/docs/memory` — notes are Markdown in the
  repo, so they stay part of the corpus.
- **Tools:** `mcp__basic-memory__write_note` / `read_note` / `search_notes` / `build_context`
  / `recent_activity`.
- **Hygiene:** gitignore the derived SQLite index; add a `.bmignore` to keep PII out of notes.

Agents MUST still work **without** it — the corpus remains the default and the source of
truth. Where this rule says "record" or "recall", the `basic-memory` tools are an optional
add-on over the same Markdown, never a replacement.

